body:
I must admit I'm a little bit surprised that this works from a functional perspective. I would've thought that making the sensor static would break its ability to be detected by other monitoring sensors, since you're not enabling `mCollideKinematicVsNonDynamic` on the area's `JPH::BodyCreationSettings`, but apparently there are special cases made for kinematic vs sensor collisions that I had missed, which allow for these collisions to be detected:

https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/c3ae6aa10b1c0c5488cf5b5e82dd34d265c1567c/thirdparty/jolt_physics/Jolt/Physics/Body/Body.inl#L35-L44

@jrouwe What exactly is the optimization at play with static sensors? Is it simply that they will be inactive/asleep and therefore won't detect any collisions between eachother? If so, would it not also be possible to achieve this by deactivating the kinematic sensors when not monitoring? A quick test seems to suggest that's the case, but I'm not sure what other side effects it might have.
